[{:db/id 17592186229163 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/id #uuid "388312be-28d6-5dd3-90fa-f6561f195420" :question/math-academy-id "q-332708" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=9{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{-6x},$ and ${e}^{-6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-6x}-6x{e}^{-6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-6x){e}^{-6x})+6\\alpha x{e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ (1-6x+6x)\\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-6x} & =9{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=9x{e}^{-6x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/id #uuid "f31cbcf7-0ca7-5dff-a5ff-a27c6b8e3c33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/id #uuid "f2b3766c-24fe-5e39-af92-4bf51a3a4723" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9xe^{-6x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229643 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=6{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228087 :answer-field/correct "answer-d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/id #uuid "d6d8db23-4013-5d8a-8bb0-41b6f6fa3c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6xe^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307604 :answer-field/correct "answer-55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/id #uuid "55f5feee-d649-5b39-a104-bdb383b1096c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(8x-1)e^{-2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307722 :answer-field/correct "answer-3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/id #uuid "3e468feb-d4ae-5e37-bcbd-d076dac5bb84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{5x}+15xe^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308275 :answer-field/correct "answer-a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/id #uuid "a6180aa2-764b-5335-9218-b9ecd269a77d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5x+A)e^{7x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-bc665116-5a76-55aa-9353-754f3071777d" :activity/id #uuid "bc665116-5a76-55aa-9353-754f3071777d" :activity/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs With Exponential Forcing" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049146 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-7a3db07d-09b8-53c0-a769-ee5a0527289c" "step-748c2caa-2098-5b2d-8ce4-acf93b07faac" "step-adfad238-f91c-58f5-8dc5-b475c8a368d7" "step-c64f98e2-0858-55ea-8d34-04c204a10d14" "step-2c91bcfc-99f4-5